.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-delete\-input\-file \-R
Deletes the file given with \fB\-\-input\fP after its swath files have been
listed. \fBmbgrdviz\fP and \fBmbgrdviz-qt\fP use it for the temporary
datalist they pass.
.TP
.B \-\-format=format_id \-Fformat_id
Sets the \fBMBIO\fP format id of the file given with \fB\-\-input\fP. By
default it is taken from the ".mbXX" suffix of the file name.
.TP
.B \-\-help \-H
Prints a description of the program and its options, then exits.
.TP
.B \-\-input=file \-Ifile
Lists the swath files of a datalist (or one swath file) as available for
editing.
.TP
.B \-\-simple\-mean\-grid \-G
Grids with the simple mean algorithm instead of the footprint algorithm.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose \-V
Prints the program version and more information about its work to the
stderr stream. Give it twice for debugging output.

.SH THE MAIN WINDOW
The status shows the number of available and loaded files and the grid.
\fBMode\fP chooses whether edits are written (Edit Bathymetry) or not (Browse
Only). \fBSwath Files\fP lists the files; a row shows <loaded> or <Locked>,
<esf> if an edit save file exists, H, S and A for asynchronous heading,
sensor depth and attitude data, the file name and the format.
\fBRemove Selected Files\fP removes the selected files from the list.
\fBView Selected Files\fP and \fBView All Files\fP load the files and open the
Grid Parameters window.

File > Open Swath Data adds a swath file or datalist: choose the output mode,
the \fBMBIO\fP format (filled in from the file name) and the file. Options >
Generate New Grid opens the Grid Parameters window again for the loaded
files. File > Quit unloads the files, writing the edits, and ends the program;
closing the window does the same.

.SH THE GRID PARAMETERS WINDOW
It shows the grid bounds and suggested parameters, and the parameters
selected with the grid cell size slider, the gridding algorithm (Simple Mean,
Footprint, Shoal Bias) and the interpolation scale in grid cells. Apply makes
the grid and shows it in the map window; Dismiss closes the window.

.SH THE 3D SOUNDINGS WINDOW
Choose the Pick Area mouse mode in the map window and drag a line across the
data, or pick a region or a navigation track, to show the soundings inside it.

The edit mode buttons set what the left mouse button does: Toggle flags or
unflags the sounding nearest the mouse, Pick flags it, Erase flags and Restore
unflags the soundings the mouse passes over, Grab flags the soundings in a
dragged rectangle, and Info shows a sounding's file, ping and beam in the
status line. The middle and right buttons rotate the soundings and change the
vertical exaggeration, or pan and zoom (Mouse menu, or the buttons under Reset
View).

Keys: u or q Toggle, i or w Pick, o or e Erase, p or r Restore, t, [ or {
Grab, y, ] or } Info. The ping keys act on the ping of the last edited
sounding: z or m flags and s or k unflags all its soundings, a or j flags its
soundings on one side of that sounding (beam numbers up to it) and d or l
those on the other side, and ! sets its soundings to null. x, < or , flags
and c, > or . unflags all soundings in view.

The View menu shows or hides the bounding box, flagged soundings and secondary
picks, draws profiles, scales with flagged soundings, and colours the
soundings by flag state, topography, sounding colour or amplitude. The Action
menu applies the bias values to the grid, flags soundings in sparse voxels,
colours the unflagged soundings and optimises the bias values. The Heading,
Roll and Pitch Bias, Time Lag and Snell Correction sliders set the values; a
slider's range moves when it reaches an end. Dismiss closes the window.

.SH DIFFERENCES FROM MBEDITVIZ
The program name in lock files and edit save files is mbeditviz-qt.

In the 3D soundings window the Mouse menu works as the mouse mode buttons,
and the Info text replaces the bias sliders while it is shown, as in
\fBmbeditviz\fP.

